{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And he said to them, Is it well with him, etc., and behold Rachel, etc.</b> The reason they did not say <b>shalom to him</b> in the language of the question is that there are two things implied in Jacob's words: first, whether he is whole in body and in property; and second, whether they are at peace with him. And the meaning of his words is, 'Is it shalom [from you] toward him?' Therefore they were cunning and said 'shalom,' meaning 'shalom' for everything you are asking about. And the reason they said <b>and behold Rachel</b> as an answer to what he did not ask is, perhaps, that in order not to prolong the conversation with him in questions, they said, Behold Rachel his daughter is coming, and it is to her that one should ask.'" "<b>Further,</b> they may have intended to say that regarding his being at peace with them — that he sends a young daughter with the sheep, something that was not so with Jethro, who would send his seven daughters, and even so the shepherds would drive them away (Exodus 2:17)." "<b>Further,</b> they intended to say to him <b>shalom</b> — meaning: regarding what pertains to his body and to his standing among us, shalom. And in order that he not understand that their intention was to say that he is wealthy and has assets, they concluded by saying <b>and behold Rachel</b>, etc. And from the inference he would know that he is not estimable in assets, for he needs his daughter to tend [the flock], and also that he lacks even a single daughter to tend his sheep, for they are few. And their intention in the precision of their words is so as not to let a false statement issue from their mouths regarding something that is destined to be revealed. Or they were concerned that Laban might say to them, 'Why did you put out a sound of wealth about him?' And for its own sake, as Scripture says (Proverbs 27:14): ״מְבָרֵךְ רֵעֵהוּ בְּקוֹל גָּדוֹל״ — so that [such blessings] not be hosted by him much and one becomes poor.'" "<b>Or</b> it is possible that their intention was to inform Jacob that Laban is a miser with his property, and degrades himself by having his precious daughter tend his sheep. And from this he would infer to know the man and his deeds."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:33.681185024Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}